WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.380
For nearly five decades, Gene Hackman was
a staple of American cinema.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:08.600
During that time, the San Bernardino-born
actor won four Golden Globes, two BAFTAs,

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.540
and two Academy Awards while starring in classic
films like Hoosiers, Unforgiven, and Get Shorty,

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.860
to name just a very few.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.760
Since the early 2000s, however, we haven't
seen or heard much from the accomplished actor

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.180
and all-American tough guy.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.820
So what's Hackman been up to?

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.400
Retired

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.160
First and foremost, Hackman is retired — which
explains why we haven't seen much of the guy

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:29.660
lately.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.820
His last role was in the 2004 romantic comedy
Welcome to Mooseport, in which he acted alongside

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.449
sitcom star Ray Romano.

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:40.300
After that, Hackman called it quits, but he
didn't really make a big deal out of it.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.340
He told Reuters,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.690
"I'm not going to act any longer.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.539
I've been told not to say that over the last
few years, in case some real wonderful part

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.880
comes up, but I really don't want to do it
any longer."

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.500
"Absolutely and without discussion!"

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.260
Novelist

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.220
Just because Hackman retired from acting doesn't
mean he's not working.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.660
In fact, he's actually penned quite a few
novels.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.580
In addition to co-writing three works of historical
fiction, Hackman has also released two solo

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.620
efforts: Old West revenge story Payback at
Morning Peak and police thriller Pursuit.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.640
These days, Hackman greatly prefers writing
to acting.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:13.920
He told Reuters,

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.880
"I like the loneliness of it, actually.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.420
It's similar in some ways to acting, but it's
more private, and I feel like I have more

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.480
control over what I'm trying to say and do."

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.400
Painter

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.760
In addition to acting and writing novels,
Hackman's other artistic pursuits include

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.720
painting — and he's pretty darn good at
it.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.040
The accomplished actor actually studied art
at the Art Students League of New York as

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:33.800
a young man.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:40.920
"I thought maybe I could feed myself as an
artist while I was waiting to be an actor"

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.480
"I'm doing something almost all the time,
drawing or painting."

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.420
He's been creating art ever since, but rarely
shows what he calls his, quote, "unshowable"

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.220
work to the public.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.600
He has, however, donated some artwork to charitable
causes.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.460
No grandpa roles

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.740
There's one pretty big reason why we'll likely
never see Hackman come out of retirement for

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.140
another big-screen role: the aging actor simply
isn't interested in playing older characters.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.800
According to ContactMusic, Hackman said,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.360
"At my age, they would have me playing grandfathers
and great grandfathers.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.920
That's not a heck of a lot of fun."

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.260
Nobody puts Hackman in a box.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:19.980
Behind the scenes

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.200
Perhaps the main reason Hackman is happy to
be done with showbiz is that he really doesn't

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.000
miss all of the behind-the-scenes nonsense
that comes with the job.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.660
He told ContactMusic,

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:30.820
"I don't miss the business.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.300
I miss the process of being on-set with actors
when things get cooking.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.120
But there's so much crapola in order to get
there.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:38.080
It's just too painful."

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.720
When you've accomplished all that Hackman
has, there's really no reason to take anyone

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.720
else's "crapola."

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.360
"If he doesn't like it, then that's his problem."

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.600
An important narration

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.860
Although Hackman doesn't lend his physical
presence to the screen anymore, he's not afraid

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.920
to lend his voice to worthwhile projects.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:59.100
Hackman sort of came back from retirement
to narrate 2016's The Unknown Flag Raiser

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.310
of Iwo Jima, a one-hour documentary aiming
to correct the misidentification of one soldier

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.260
in Joe Rosenthal's famous World War 2 photograph.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:12.460
"The picture conceals an extraordinary case
of mistaken identity."

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.500
Hackman himself dropped out of high school
at the age of 16 to enlist in the Marine Corps,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.500
where he served as a field radio operator
for over four years.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.060
"He's a ballsy little bastard isn't he?"

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.380
Will he act again?

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:27.860
Well, probably not.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.700
Hackman seems quite satisfied with the arc
of his award-filled career.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.660
Speaking of which, the actor seems to have
distanced himself so much from that part of

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.340
his life, he may not even know where his awards
are.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:39.980
He told GQ,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.260
"I don't have any memorabilia around the house.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.280
There isn't any movie stuff except a poster
downstairs next to the pool table of Errol

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.960
Flynn from Dawn Patrol.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.660
I'm not a sentimental guy."

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.960
Still, there is one role that could possibly
entice Hackman out of retirement.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.340
He explained,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.220
"I like Edmond in The Count of Monte Cristo.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.800
[...] Having been able to keep that terrible
vengeance in his soul for so many years and

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:03.659
then carrying out what he thought were justified
events in the end.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.620
I like that as a novel."

00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:06.180
Thanks for watching!

